  "question": {
    "text": "In a triangle $\\mathrm{PQR}, \\angle \\mathrm{R}=\\frac{\\pi}{2}$. If $\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right)$ and $\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)$ are the roots of $a x^2+b x+c=0, a \\neq 0$ then",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "ljq6i",
      "text": "\n$a=b+c$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "Zk5HI",
      "text": "\n$c=a+b$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "WqnzY",
      "text": "\n$b=c$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "7Hwo6",
      "text": "\n$b=a+c$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right), \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)$ are the roots of $\\mathrm{ax}^2+\\mathrm{bx}+\\mathrm{c}=0$\n$$\n\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right)+\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)=-\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}}\n$$\n$\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right) \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)=\\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}}$\n$\\frac{\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right)+\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)}{1-\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}\\right) \\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)}=\\tan \\left(\\frac{\\mathrm{P}}{2}+\\frac{\\mathrm{Q}}{2}\\right)=1$\n$\\Rightarrow \\frac{-\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}}}{1-\\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}}}=1 \\Rightarrow-\\frac{\\mathrm{b}}{\\mathrm{a}}=\\frac{\\mathrm{a}}{\\mathrm{a}}-\\frac{\\mathrm{c}}{\\mathrm{a}} \\Rightarrow-\\mathrm{b}=\\mathrm{a}-\\mathrm{c}$\n$\\mathrm{c}=\\mathrm{a}+\\mathrm{b}$.",
    "image": null
  },
